ord-format: "0.1"
---

# Assumptions

**Acme Services \| strategic valuation, December 2026**

Amounts in $000 and shares in 000; per-share values are in dollars. Management expects growth to moderate as the service network matures. The base case uses an 8% annual sales increase and a 20% operating margin.

The financing bridge includes $4.6m debt and $1.6m cash. The fully diluted share count is shown separately from the basic equity value.

| Input                 || Value                                              |
| --------------------- || -------------------------------------------------- |
| Base revenue          || 10000 {:,.0f} {::>}                                |
| Revenue growth        || 0.08 {:.1%}                                        |
| EBIT margin           || 0.2 {:.1%}                                         |
| Tax rate              || 0.25 {:.1%}                                        |
| Depreciation ratio    || 0.03 {:.1%}                                        |
| Capex ratio           || 0.05 {:.1%}                                        |
| Working capital ratio || 0.1 {:.1%}                                         |
| WACC                  || 0.1 {:.1%}                                         |
| Terminal growth       || 0.025 {:.1%}                                       |
| Net debt              || 3000 = @["Gross debt",_] - @[Cash,_] {:,.0f} {::>} |
| Shares outstanding    || 1000 {:,.0f} {::>}                                 |
| Forecast gross margin || 0.5 {:.1%}                                         |
| Cash                  || 1600 {:,.0f} {::>}                                 |
| Gross debt            || 4600 {:,.0f} {::>}                                 |
| Valuation date        || 2026-12-31 {:%Y-%m-%d}                             |
| Diluted share count   || 1050 {:,.0f} {::>}                                 |



# Forecast

**Historical results and seven-year unlevered forecast**

2024–2026 are management actuals; 2027–2033 form the forecast. Working capital is 10% of revenue, and only its annual increase is charged against cash flow. Cash flows are valued at year-end.

The terminal case assumes a mature operation: revenue growth slows to 2.5%, while replacement capital and working-capital needs continue.

